verdicts.fixes:
1. Reposition novelty honestly. Cite Pellegrino et al. 2023 as the origin of low-tensor-rank learning, and Williams et al. TCA plus arXiv 2405.03244 for the activation-space analogue. State the new contribution as: (a) a sparse, overcomplete, causally refined CP-over-training for transformers (CI-masked, KL-faithful); (b) identifiability measured empirically, not asserted; (c) a mechanism × data × time ledger in that basis; and (d) recovery of transient 'fossil' mechanisms invisible to any final-checkpoint method. (d) is the clearest unique selling point: per-checkpoint and final-checkpoint baselines structurally cannot find components with g(T) ≈ 0.

2. Replace the generic-identifiability rhetoric with a quantitative identifiability theory that can be tested.
- Report the numerical k-rank and effective temporal rank of the fitted time-factor matrix, for example the minimum singular value over subsets.
- Derive the predicted identifiability frontier from Kruskal and Jennrich: R versus d versus temporal k-rank.
- Make the D1 phase diagram's axes 'superposition ratio × measured temporal k-rank' (onset-time spread), and pre-register that DPD's advantage over final-checkpoint SPD appears where k-rank is high and vanishes where it is collapsed.
- Include an unconstrained (non-spline) CP variant so you can show what the smooth prior costs and what it buys.

3. Fix the ablations. Replace 'shuffle checkpoint order' with:
- temporal collapse: T = 1 (final only), T = 2, and T = 2 at mid-training;
- forced collinearity: synthetic trajectories where all planted components share one g(t);
- an onset-spread sweep;
- an order-shuffle kept separately, labelled as a prior-misspecification control.

4. Add mandatory baselines:
- (i) per-checkpoint VPD with cross-time matching;
- (ii) final-checkpoint VPD plus projection of ΔW(t) and gradients onto its subcomponents (Goodfire's §7.5 recipe);
- (iii) rolling-window update/gradient SVD (Spectral Edge, 2603.28964 and 2604.25143);
- (iv) for the ledger, POLCA (Hessian basis), ExPLAIND (parameter/layer influence), and crosscoders-through-time on the same TinyStories run.
DPD must beat these on held-out per-token loss-curve prediction and on ablation-validated birth times, not just on MMCS.

5. Establish headroom before building. As step 0, run param-decomp SPD/VPD on the planted toys across 5 seeds and superposition ratios 1 to 8, and publish their MMCS and seed stability. If the baselines do not fall below 0.7, retarget D1 toward transient and fossil recovery and birth-time accuracy, where final-checkpoint methods fail by construction. Also build a planted-transient toy: a curriculum where a task's data is removed mid-training so its mechanism is dismantled.

6. Reframe H2 as testing the Quantization Model's training-time discreteness against a continuous-growth null, for example sum-of-sigmoids against a smooth power law or a Gaussian-process null on per-component g(t) and per-token loss. Use model comparison with held-out likelihood. Drop the claim that it adjudicates against the superposition-scaling paper. Add a test of the quanta prediction that birth order follows usage frequency, with the gradient-clustering QDG of 2303.13506 as the comparison.

7. Harden D2. Don't count Fourier alignment as a success, since final-checkpoint SVD gets it. Pre-register instead:
- memorization components detected and validated by ablation at their peak checkpoint;
- their death time matching Nanda's cleanup phase in excluded loss;
- the ledger share of Fourier components tracking the restricted-loss curve, with a correlation threshold;
- comparison against ExPLAIND's phase account and the tensor-similarity curves.

8. Address optimizer artifacts directly. Fit on weight states with an explicit a(t)·W(0) decay. Also report a variant fit on accumulated loss gradients rather than AdamW deltas (following 2604.25143), and add a regularised per-component direction drift u_c(t) with a reported drift budget. Call the ledger an 'approximate path-integral allocation' and report the residual fraction per window.

9. De-risk scope. Ship Stage 1 plus the projection baseline plus fossil detection on toys and grokking first, since that is data-free and runs on CPU. Make Stage 2 (t-conditioned CI with VPD) the second milestone. Treat Pythia-14m/70m with public revisions (log-spaced steps 0 to 512, then every 1k) as the real-LM test, and leave TinyStories self-training optional.

10. Interoperate with interp-lab by exporting components and their per-token CI as feature records for causal grading. Position ASPD (2609.37731) as an orthogonal identifiability route, activation grounding against temporal grounding, that could be combined with DPD.
